{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Mentor Teacher Conversation Guides","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting mentor teacher conversation guides to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"02","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/01"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Mentor Teacher Conversation Guides\n\n![Mentor Teacher Conversation Guides textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-02-mentor-teacher-conversation-guides.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA mentor teacher holds something you do not: standing, history with the students, and accountability if a tool goes wrong. When you want to try a technology the mentor has restricted, the conversation is not a debate to win; it is a negotiation between two people with different information and unequal authority. A conversation guide is the artifact that turns that negotiation from a complaint into a professional inquiry. It records what you asked, what concern the mentor actually held, what you agreed to, and what you ruled out together.\n\nThis section treats the guide as a map of a classroom value conflict and the decision you negotiated around it. The skill is not persuading the mentor; it is surfacing the real reason behind a policy so the eventual decision protects students rather than just satisfying you.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Reframe a request to a mentor from a grievance into an inquiry that can surface the policy's actual rationale.\n- Distinguish the stated rule (no phones) from the underlying concern it protects (uncontrolled student-data exposure).\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with the safeguard you accepted and the use you ruled out.\n- Write a conversation guide a third party could read to see the concern, the agreed safeguard, and the planned check.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nWhen a mentor says \"no phones in my room,\" the rule is a proxy for a concern, and you do not yet know which concern. It could be off-task behavior, an incident with parents, a district directive, or a specific app that mishandled student data last year. Arguing against the rule treats it as arbitrary. Asking about the rule treats the mentor as a professional with reasons you have not heard yet.\n\nThe conversation guide records that difference. A weak guide scripts your case for why you should get what other candidates get. A strong guide scripts the question that lets the mentor name the real risk, because only once the risk is named can the two of you design a use that respects it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvery classroom rule is a compressed judgment. \"No phones\" compresses a history of incidents, a read of these particular students, and a tolerance for risk into a single prohibition. The conversation guide decompresses it. The central technique is moving from positions to interests: a position is what someone demands (the ban, or your request to use a polling app), and an interest is the underlying concern the position protects (keeping student responses off an unvetted server, getting fast formative data).\n\nWhen two positions collide, there is rarely a winner. When the interests beneath them are named, a third option often appears that serves both. In the phone case, the position \"no phones\" and the position \"let me use a polling app\" look irreconcilable until the mentor's interest (no student data on an outside server) surfaces; then a district-approved version that keeps responses on the school server satisfies both interests at once.\n\nThree lenses keep the conversation defensible:\n\n- **TPACK** keeps the request anchored to a learning purpose. If you cannot say what the polling app does for the content that pencil-and-paper cannot, the mentor is right to refuse it.\n- **Critical edtech studies** names whose interests a tool serves beyond the classroom. The mentor's worry about an outside server is exactly this lens applied: the app's vendor has interests in the data that your lesson does not.\n- **Teacher professional judgment** treats the mentor's accumulated reasons as data, not as obstacles. A policy you cannot yet explain is a gap in your information, not proof the policy is wrong.\n\nA lens has done its work when it changes what you propose, not just how you argue for what you already wanted.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A mentor says 'no phones in my room.' In positions-and-interests terms, what is 'no phones'?\" explanation=\"'No phones' is a position: the demand someone states. The interest is the underlying concern it protects, which you do not yet know (off-task behavior, a past data incident, a district directive). Surfacing that interest is what lets a third option appear that serves both sides.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A position that compresses an unstated interest you have not surfaced yet\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"An interest, because it names exactly what the mentor is worried about\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"An arbitrary rule you should argue against directly\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two openers for the same request. The grievance version frames the mentor as an obstacle:\n\n> Your phone ban is outdated. Other candidates get to use phones, so I should too.\n\nThis guarantees a defensive answer and surfaces no information. The inquiry version frames the mentor as a source:\n\n> Can you walk me through the reason behind the phone policy? I have one bounded use I would like to think through with you.\n\nThe second opener does two things the first cannot. It invites the mentor to name the interest beneath the rule, and it signals that your use is bounded (one use, not a general license). The negotiated decision that follows can then be specific: the district-approved app for one ungraded warm-up, responses on the school server, no named student data collected. That specificity is what makes the guide auditable later.\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Negotiating a Phone Policy With a Mentor\n\nDevon wants to try a free polling app for quick formative checks, but his mentor bans all student phone use. He drafts a conversation guide.\n\n1. **Before (complaint draft):** \"Your phone ban is outdated. Other candidates get to use phones, so I should too.\"\n2. **Reframe to inquiry:** He rewrites the opener as a question: \"Can you walk me through the reason behind the phone policy? I have one bounded use I would like to think through with you.\"\n3. **Mentor's actual concern surfaced:** The mentor explains that last year a similar app stored student responses on an outside server with no district agreement. Devon records this as the real constraint, not an obstacle.\n4. **Negotiated decision (after):** \"We agreed I may use the polling app for one ungraded warm-up, but only the district-approved version that keeps responses on the school server. We ruled out collecting any named student data.\"\n5. **Check during the lesson:** Devon will confirm no names are entered and report back whether the warm-up surfaced misconceptions worth reteaching.\n\nThe record shows the concern, the agreed safeguard, the use ruled out, and the check. That is professional judgment, not just getting a yes.\n\nNow sit in the conversation yourself. Each turn either surfaces the mentor's real interest or hardens the standoff.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Negotiating a phone policy with your mentor\",\"start\":\"open\",\"nodes\":{\"open\":{\"prompt\":\"You want to try a free polling app for quick formative checks. Your mentor bans all student phone use. How do you open?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Your phone ban is outdated; other candidates get to use phones, so I should too\",\"next\":\"grievance\",\"feedback\":\"You framed the mentor as an obstacle. This surfaces no information.\"},{\"label\":\"Can you walk me through the reason behind the phone policy? I have one bounded use I would like to think through with you\",\"next\":\"inquiry\",\"feedback\":\"You framed the mentor as a source and signaled a bounded use.\"}]},\"grievance\":{\"prompt\":\"The mentor responds defensively and the conversation closes. What did you learn about the rule?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; the interest stayed hidden\",\"next\":\"closed\",\"feedback\":\"A position-versus-position clash with no interests named.\"}]},\"closed\":{\"prompt\":\"You got a no and never learned the real concern. Restart with inquiry.\",\"feedback\":\"Arguing positions with someone who holds authority loses. Ask for the rationale first.\",\"choices\":[]},\"inquiry\":{\"prompt\":\"The mentor explains: last year a similar app stored student responses on an outside server with no district agreement. What do you do with this?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Record it as the real constraint, then propose a use that addresses it\",\"next\":\"safeguard\",\"feedback\":\"You treated the mentor reason as data, not an obstacle.\"},{\"label\":\"Argue that this app is different and probably fine\",\"next\":\"closed\",\"feedback\":\"You dismissed the surfaced interest instead of designing around it.\"}]},\"safeguard\":{\"prompt\":\"How do you propose using the app so it addresses the outside-server concern?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The district-approved version for one ungraded warm-up, responses on the school server, no named data\",\"next\":\"agreement\",\"feedback\":\"Your safeguard maps directly onto the mentor actual interest.\"},{\"label\":\"Any version, but you promise to be careful with the data\",\"next\":\"closed\",\"feedback\":\"A promise is not a safeguard; it does not address the server mechanism.\"}]},\"agreement\":{\"prompt\":\"You agree on one bounded use with a safeguard and a check (confirm no names entered, report whether the warm-up surfaced misconceptions). This is agreement, not just permission.\",\"feedback\":\"You surfaced the interest, named a bounded use, and proposed a safeguard that resolved the mentor concern.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A mentor refuses to let you use an AI essay-feedback tool because last year it flattened the voice of multilingual writers and a parent complained. Your interest is faster feedback. State a bounded use plus a safeguard that serves both interests at once.\" answer=\"Bound the AI to flag only mechanics while you handle voice and content. Add the safeguard that any flagged work from multilingual students is reviewed by you before any AI comment is shown to the student. This serves your interest (faster feedback on mechanics) and the mentor interest (protecting multilingual students voice), and it is specific enough to record and audit later.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nIn a mentor conversation, surface the interest before you defend the position. The order is: ask for the rationale first, name the bounded use you want second, and propose a safeguard that addresses the rationale third. Devon's case follows it exactly: he asked first (the server concern surfaced), named one bounded use (a single ungraded warm-up), and proposed the safeguard (district-approved version, no named data) that resolved the mentor's actual interest.\n\nBefore you treat a negotiated decision as final, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Interest check:** Can you state the concern beneath the mentor's rule in the mentor's own terms, not as \"they just don't like phones\"?\n2. **Safeguard check:** Does your proposed use address that specific concern, or does it only address your convenience?\n3. **Boundary check:** What did you and the mentor explicitly rule out, and what check will you run during the lesson to confirm the boundary held?\n\nA \"yes\" without these is permission, not agreement. The guide should record agreement.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating a \"yes\" as the goal. Getting permission while leaving the mentor's concern unaddressed sets up the next incident. The second is mistaking engagement for justification: \"students love the app\" is a position, not a reason that answers a data-safety interest. The third is reading the mentor's policy as ignorance to be corrected. Mentors usually hold reasons you have not heard; a guide that treats those reasons as obstacles has skipped the only step that makes the conversation professional.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the positions-to-interests move to a different conflict. A mentor refuses to let you use an AI essay-feedback tool. Your position is \"let me use it to give faster feedback.\" The mentor's position is \"no AI on student writing.\" If you argue the positions, you lose, because the mentor has authority and you have a grievance.\n\nInstead, ask for the rationale. Suppose the mentor's interest is that AI feedback last year flattened the voice of multilingual writers and a parent complained. Now the conflict reframes: your interest (faster feedback) and the mentor's interest (protecting multilingual students' voice) can both be served by a bounded use where the AI flags only mechanics while you handle voice and content, with the multilingual students' flagged work reviewed by you before any AI comment is shown. The guide records the surfaced interest, the bounded use, and the review safeguard. The walkthrough is the same structure as the phone case with a different concern; the technique transfers, the specifics do not.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a conversation guide, keep a two-column note: **position** on the left, **interest** on the right. For every demand on either side, write the concern it protects. A guide that has only positions and no interests is a script for an argument; a guide with interests is a map for a decision. The presence of named interests on both sides is the signal that the candidate negotiated rather than lobbied.\n\nA strong reader of a conversation guide can answer four questions:\n\n1. What concern does the mentor's rule actually protect, in the mentor's terms?\n2. What bounded use did the candidate propose, and how narrow is the boundary?\n3. What safeguard addresses the mentor's specific concern, not the candidate's convenience?\n4. What would the candidate renegotiate if the mentor's concern turned out to be different than assumed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA conversation guide is strong when a reader who was not in the room can see both the concern and the agreement. That requires the guide to record the mentor's interest in concrete terms (last year's outside-server incident, not \"the mentor is cautious\"), the specific bounded use agreed to, and the use explicitly ruled out. Smooth phrasing is the last layer; a guide that reads as a persuasive essay but never names the mentor's real concern has documented a debate, not a negotiation.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like this: \"We agreed to a single bounded use because it addressed the mentor's actual concern about an outside server, and we ruled out named-data collection entirely. I rejected the version that was faster for me, because it would have reintroduced exactly the risk the policy exists to prevent.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same negotiation skill to a higher-stakes setting: a mentor who is not cautious but enthusiastic, who wants you to use a new AI grading tool you have doubts about. Now the authority gradient runs the other way; the mentor is pushing the tool and you hold the concern. The positions-to-interests move still applies, but you are the one who must surface an interest (consistency of grading across students, your own ability to defend a grade) without dismissing the mentor's enthusiasm. If you can run the same negotiation when the power dynamic reverses, you understand the technique rather than one comfortable script.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-02-mentor-teacher-conversation-guides-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 07.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your guide records the mentor's actual concern, the bounded use agreed to, the use explicitly ruled out, and the check you will run during the lesson. That is what separates a negotiation from a request for permission.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides","stem":"A candidate is drafting a conversation guide to raise a disagreement with her mentor, who bans all student phone use including a free polling app the candidate wants to try. Which opening line keeps the guide a professional negotiation rather than a complaint?","options":["I think your phone ban is outdated and hurts engagement.","I would like to understand the reason behind the phone policy, then share one bounded use I am considering and the safeguard I would add.","Other mentor teachers let candidates use phones, so I should be allowed to as well.","Can I just use the app once without telling the class it is against the rules?"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible guide opens by surfacing the mentor's reasoning, then proposes one bounded use with a safeguard. The other lines attack the policy, appeal to other classrooms, or hide the decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_guide_as_persuasion_script","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The mentor says the polling app worried her because last year a similar app stored student responses on an outside server. What should the candidate add to the conversation guide in response?","options":["A note that the mentor is being too cautious about a free tool.","A question that names the real concern: where student responses are stored and whether the district has approved the vendor.","A reminder that the candidate only needs to use it for one lesson.","A statement that students like the app, so the concern is minor."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong guide records the mentor's actual concern (data storage and vendor approval) as a question to resolve together, not as an obstacle to argue past.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_concern_as_obstacle","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate asks an AI tool to draft the whole mentor conversation, including the mentor's likely replies. How should she use the output?","options":["Read the AI script aloud in the meeting so the conversation stays on track.","Use the AI prompts as a starting list, then revise them around what she actually knows about this mentor's stated values and prior comments.","Trust the AI's predicted mentor replies because they sound reasonable.","Let the AI decide which classroom rule to challenge first."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A real mentor conversation depends on this person's stated values, which AI cannot know. The AI draft is a starting list to revise, not a script to perform.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_ai_scripts_the_mentor","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_mentor_teacher_conversation_guides_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"After the conversation, what record best shows the candidate exercised professional judgment rather than just getting permission?","options":["A note that the mentor said yes.","A summary of the mentor's concern, the safeguard they agreed on, the one use they ruled out, and how the candidate will check it during the lesson.","A copy of the polling app's marketing page.","A note of how long the meeting lasted."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Professional judgment is visible in the negotiated boundary: the concern, the agreed safeguard, the ruled-out use, and the check. A yes alone does not show reasoning.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_2_permission_equals_judgment","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]}}